# G042 — Whois Search Tool

*v2. See `whois-search.md` for prose: ownership as network-identity
provenance, registration as temporal lease, domain-specific regex
parsing over non-standard formats, record comparison as change
detection.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Domain Health Monitoring

[domain-health @domains
    IN ORDER TO
        query whois for each tracked domain concurrently
        flag upcoming expiries and detect unexpected registration changes
        so compromises or lapsing leases surface before they break DNS

    concurrent
        @records <- @domains mapped concurrently to
                     [whois[query {domain: @d}]]
    join

    @expiring <- @records
        where days-until-expiry is less than 30
    @changed  <- @domains mapped to
        [whois[compare
                 {before: [history[last-record {domain: @d}]],
                  after:  @records where domain is @d first}]]

    @changed where changes count is greater than zero
        -> #whois-changed {domain, changes}

    @expiring where count is greater than zero
        -> #expiry-soon {domains: @expiring mapped to domain}

    @records
        where any name-server does not contain "digitalocean"
            -> #unexpected-nameservers (possible DNS hijack)
]

---

## Verb Surface

[whois
    query           {domain}                   ;; -> WhoisRecord | unknown
    parse           {raw-text}                  ;; regex-per-field
    compare         {before, after}             ;; -> {changes: [kind]}
    days-until-expiry {record}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: flexible parse `where` field name varies

[whois[parse {raw-text: @t}]
    IN ORDER TO
        extract fields across TLDs and registrars where the same concept
        appears under different names ("Creation Date" vs "Created Date"
        vs "Creat Date") without failing on missing or reordered fields

    @t searched line-by-line
        where line matches /Registrar:\s*(.+)/i      -> registrar := group 1
        where line matches /Creat(ion|ed)? Date:\s*(.+)/i
                                                       -> created := group 2
        where line matches /(Expir(y|ation)|Registry Expiry) Date:\s*(.+)/i
                                                       -> expiry := group 3
        where line matches /Name Server:\s*(.+)/i     -> append to name-servers
        otherwise                                      -> skip
]

Real-world unstandardised text formats demand multiple patterns per
field. G032's regex toolkit applied to domain registration — the
resolver's equivalent when natural-language inputs express the same
concept in many surface forms.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: change as `where` field differs between snapshots

[whois[compare {before: @a, after: @b}]
    changes <- []
        where @a:registrar is not @b:registrar
            -> append #registrar-changed (possible domain transfer)
        where @a:name-servers is not @b:name-servers
            -> append #nameservers-changed (possible DNS hijack)
        where @a:expiry-date is not @b:expiry-date
            -> append #expiry-changed (renewal or lapse)
        where @a:status is not @b:status
            -> append #status-changed (lock/unlock)
    IN ORDER TO
        classify each mutation by its operational meaning
        so the alert path knows whether this is maintenance or a compromise
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @r <- [whois[query {domain: "eckenrodemuziekopname.com"}]]
    -> @r:registrar                            ;; -> "DigitalOcean" or similar
    -> [whois[days-until-expiry {record: @r}]]  ;; -> e.g. 183

    @old <- @r with name-servers replaced by ["ns1.attacker.com"]
    -> [whois[compare {before: @r, after: @old}]]
    ;; -> {changes: [#nameservers-changed]}
]
